    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Fustat began in 641 as a garrison camp founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amr-ibn-al-as">Amr ibn al-As</a> after the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, and it served as the base for Arab expansion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> until Qayrawan was founded in 670.</li><li>It grew to about 120,000 people in the 9th century and about 200,000 by 1168, and it was a major centre of Islamic art and ceramics from 975 to 1075.</li><li>Shawar, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> of the Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, ordered it burned in 1168 to keep it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amalric-i-of-jerusalem">Amalric of Jerusalem</a>, and the centre of government then moved permanently to nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-maqrizi">al-Maqrizi</a>, the fire that Shawar ordered in 1168, set with 20,000 naphtha pots and 10,000 lighting bombs, burned for 54 days.</p></aside>
    </section>

