    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">641</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amr-ibn-al-as">Amr ibn al-As</a> founds Fustat after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">642</span><span class="rf-text">The Mosque of Amr is built on the site of the commander's tent</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">750</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> take power and move the capital north to al-Askar</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">905</span><span class="rf-text">Al-Qatta'i is destroyed and the capital returns to Fustat</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">969</span><span class="rf-text">Gawhar founds <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> just north of Fustat for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1168</span><span class="rf-text">Shawar orders Fustat burned as Amalric's army approaches</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1169</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> becomes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid dynasty</a> begins</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great.jpg" alt="Alexander the Great" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexander_Mosaic_detail_of_Alexander_the_Great_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For thousands of years the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> moved up and down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> with the dynasties, to places such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a> and Memphis. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> conquered Egypt around 331 BCE the capital was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast, and it remained so for nearly a thousand years. After the army of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> Umar captured the region in the 7th century, shortly after the death of Muhammad, a new capital was wanted. When Alexandria fell in September 641, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amr-ibn-al-as">Amr ibn al-As</a>, the commander of the conquering army, founded one on the eastern bank of the river.</p><p>According to legend a dove laid an egg in Amr's tent just before he marched against Alexandria. His camp lay just north of the Roman fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>. He declared the nest a sign from God and left the tent untouched, and when his troops returned victorious he told them to pitch their tents around his. The city took its name from this, Misr al-Fustat or Fustat Misr, popularly translated as 'city of the tents', though this is not an exact translation. The first mosque in Egypt, the Mosque of Amr, was built in 642 on the site of the commander's tent.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The early population consisted almost entirely of soldiers and their families, and the city was laid out like a garrison. Amr meant it to be a base from which to conquer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> and to launch further campaigns against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, and it remained the primary base for Arab expansion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> until Qayrawan was founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> in 670. In the 9th century Fustat was a major city with a population of approximately 120,000.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> to the north the city thrived and grew. In 987 the geographer Ibn Hawkal wrote that Fustat was approximately one third the size of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>, and by 1168 it had a population of 200,000. From 975 to 1075 it was a major production centre for Islamic art and ceramics and one of the wealthiest cities in the world. One report stated that it paid taxes equivalent to US$150,000 a day to the administration of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> al-Mu'izz. Excavations have turned up trade goods from as far away as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Fustat was the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> for approximately 500 years. Its authority was uninterrupted until 750, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid dynasty</a> revolted against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a>. Under the Umayyads, who headed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> from 660 to 750, Egypt was a province ruled by governors appointed from other Muslim centres such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. The Abbasids, who made Baghdad their centre, moved the Egyptian capital slightly north to their own city of al-Askar, which remained the capital until 868.</p><p>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tulunid-dynasty">Tulunid dynasty</a> took control in 868 the capital moved briefly to another northern city, al-Qatta'i. It lasted only until 905, when al-Qatta'i was destroyed and the capital was returned to Fustat. In 969 the Fatimid general Gawhar captured the region and founded Al Qahira (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>) just north of Fustat on 8 August, and in 971 the caliph al-Mu'izz moved his court there from al-Mansuriya in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>. Cairo served at first mainly as a royal enclosure for the caliph, his court and his army, while Fustat remained the capital in economic and administrative power. The capital of Egypt is said to have moved to Cairo in 973.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Fustat developed as a series of tribal areas, called khittas, around the central mosque and the administrative buildings. Most of the first settlers came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, the next largest group came from western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, and there were some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>. Arabic was generally the primary spoken dialect in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and the language of written communication, though Coptic was still spoken in Fustat in the 8th century.</p><p>The city was known for its prosperity, with shaded streets, gardens and markets. It had high-rise residential buildings, some seven storeys tall, which could reportedly accommodate hundreds of people. In the 10th century al-Muqaddasi described them as minarets, and in the early 11th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasir-khusraw">Nasir Khusraw</a> described some as rising up to 14 storeys, with roof gardens on the top storey and ox-drawn <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/water-wheel">water wheels</a> for irrigation. The Persian traveller Nasir-i-Khusron wrote of the wares in the markets, among them iridescent pottery, crystal and many fruits and flowers, even in winter. Excavations have revealed house and street plans in which a basic unit was a set of rooms built around a central courtyard, entered through an arcade on one side.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/amalric-i-of-jerusalem"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amalric-i-of-jerusalem.jpg" alt="Amalric I of Jerusalem" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/amalric-i-of-jerusalem">Amalric I of Jerusalem</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amalric_receives_a_message_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the mid-12th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> was the teenager Athid, whose position was primarily ceremonial. The real power was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> Shawar, who for years shifted his alliances between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> and the forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nur-al-din">Nur al-Din</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, keeping each from attacking Egypt. In 1168 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amalric-i-of-jerusalem">Amalric I of Jerusalem</a> entered Egypt with his army, sacked Bilbeis and slaughtered nearly all of its inhabitants, then camped just south of Fustat and told the caliph to surrender the city or suffer the same fate.</p><p>Shawar ordered Fustat burned to keep it out of Amalric's hands. According to the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-maqrizi">al-Maqrizi</a> (1346 to 1442), he ordered the city evacuated and the citizens forced to leave their money and property behind, and he distributed 20,000 naphtha pots and 10,000 lighting bombs through it; the blaze raged for 54 days. Syrian forces then arrived and repelled Amalric, and they went on to conquer Egypt themselves. Shawar was put to death. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a>, from the 13th to the 16th century, the area was used as a rubbish dump, though thousands still lived there, mainly potters and rubbish collectors. The layers of garbage built up over hundreds of years and the population gradually fell.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/saladin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saladin.jpg" alt="Saladin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al-Nasir_I_Salah_al-Din_Yusuf_(Saladin)._AH_564-589_(1169-1193_CE)_%C3%86_Dirham_(30.1mm,_13.28_g,_6h)._Without_mint-name._Dated_AH_586_(AD_1190-91)._Sultan_sitting_facing,_cross-legged,_on_high-backed_throne_(obverse).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Classical Numismatic Group (CNG), CC BY-SA 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After the fire Fustat was no more than a dying suburb, and the centre of government moved permanently to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>, who became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> on 2 March 1169 and began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid dynasty</a>, later tried to unite Cairo and Fustat in one city by enclosing both in massive walls, which was largely unsuccessful. Fustat, al-Askar and al-Qatta'i were all absorbed into the growing city of Cairo.</p><p>The physician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maimonides">Maimonides</a> went to Egypt in 1166 and settled in Fustat. He practised in the families of Saladin and his vizier and of Saladin's successors, and wrote his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mishneh-torah">Mishneh Torah</a> there in 1180 and The Guide for the Perplexed. Some of his writings were later found among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> fragments in the geniza of the Ben Ezra Synagogue in Fustat.</p><p>Fustat is today a suburb within the Old Cairo district. The Mosque of Amr is still in use, but it has been extensively rebuilt over the centuries and nothing remains of the original structure. The paths of streets are visible, some buildings have been partly reconstructed to waist height, and finds from the site are in Cairo's Museum of Islamic Art. The remains of the original city are believed to lie preserved under centuries of rubbish.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Fustat was founded in 641 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amr-ibn-al-as">Amr ibn al-As</a> as the first capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> under Muslim rule. It grew to about 200,000 people and was a centre of ceramics and trade until its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> Shawar had it burned in 1168. It is now a suburb within Old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>.</em></p>

